+### Leaderboard native addon: built, compiled, and verified for real -- Windows build is the one remaining blocker (patch 0379)
+```discord-update
+Dev Update | Leaderboards
+
+- The leaderboard addon discussed since patch 0363 is now real, working code -- built as a fork of steamworks.js itself, not a separate addon
+- Compiles clean, produces a genuine working Linux build, confirmed wired to the real Steam API
+- One thing left before it can actually ship: a Windows build, which needs a real Windows machine or CI
+```
+
+Direct continuation of the custom-addon decision from patch 0363/0377's
+"which way on leaderboards" discussion -- "Custom native addon now
+(steamworks-rs based, Windows + Linux only since macOS is out)."
+
+**Corrected the architecture before writing any code.** A genuinely
+separate second native addon (the original framing) would have called
+its own `SteamAPI_Init()` independently of the existing `steamworks.js`
+dependency already wired in for achievements/DLC -- confirmed via search
+that two independent inits in one process is a real, documented failure
+mode ("Tried to Initialize the SteamAPI twice in one session!"), not a
+theoretical risk this doc had previously only flagged as "I genuinely
+don't know." Built as a **fork of `steamworks.js` itself** instead --
+its own maintainer explicitly points people at exactly this for missing
+features ("You can already use steamworks-rs, which is what this
+library is based on"), and existing forks doing the same thing were
+found for precedent. One native module in the whole process, one
+`init()` call, the leaderboard functions are just new methods sharing
+the same already-initialized client every other module in that source
+tree already uses (`crate::client::get_client()`).
+
+**Confirmed the underlying Rust crate already had everything needed,
+before assuming a from-scratch implementation was necessary.** The
+`steamworks` crate `steamworks.js` itself is built on already has full
+`find_or_create_leaderboard`/`upload_leaderboard_score`/
+`download_leaderboard_entries` support -- checked docs.rs directly, then
+confirmed by actually compiling a throwaway project against it (no
+Valve SDK download needed; `steamworks-sys` vendors what it needs).
+
+**Scoped to what this game actually needs, not a generic multi-
+leaderboard API** -- caught a design assumption from patch 0363 that
+turned out wrong once the real API was read properly: Global and
+Friends are a *download-time scope* (`LeaderboardDataRequest`) on ONE
+leaderboard, not two separately-created boards. `src/api/leaderboard.rs`
+reflects that: a single `GUILD_POWER` board, cached Rust-side after the
+first `find_or_create` rather than passed back to JS at all (the Steam
+`Leaderboard` handle's inner field isn't `pub` in steamworks-rs -- no
+public way to reconstruct one from a raw value across the JS boundary,
+so it never needs to cross it in the first place).
+
+**Every claim here is backed by something actually run, not just
+written:**
+- `cargo build` against the fork's own pinned `steamworks-rs` revision
+  (not just a generic crates.io version) -- clean, no errors.
+- `nm` on the resulting `.so` confirms `FindOrCreateLeaderboard`/
+  `UploadLeaderboardScore`/`DownloadLeaderboardEntries` show up as the
+  expected external symbols the real Steam API provides at runtime --
+  wired to the genuine SDK, not a stub.
+- A full **production build via the real `napi build` toolchain**
+  succeeded, producing an actual working
+  `steamworksjs.linux-x64-gnu.node` and auto-generating correct
+  TypeScript types (`leaderboard.uploadGuildPowerScore`,
+  `GuildPowerScope`, `GuildPowerEntry` all show up correctly camelCased
+  in the regenerated `client.d.ts`, doc comments carried through as
+  JSDoc).
+
+**Deliberately NOT wired into `electron/main.ts` yet -- a real
+regression risk was caught before shipping it, not after.** Swapping
+the existing npm `steamworks.js` dependency for this fork today would
+break Windows entirely, not just leaderboards: the fork only has a
+Linux build, `index.js`'s platform loader has no fallback for a missing
+platform `.node`, and that `require()` happens at module-import time,
+before the existing try/catch (which only wraps `.init()`) ever runs.
+Achievements and DLC currently work fine on Windows via the real npm
+package -- swapping prematurely would silently break both the moment
+this shipped, not just leave leaderboards unavailable. Held back
+explicitly rather than risk that.
+
+**What's actually left:** a real Windows build (needs an actual Windows
+machine or CI -- not something this sandboxed environment can produce,
+no MSVC toolchain available and cross-compiling a native napi module to
+Windows from Linux is fragile even with one), then the `package.json`
+swap, three new IPC handlers in `electron/main.ts`, and
+`src/game/leaderboard.ts`'s stub (`LEADERBOARD_READY` still `false`)
+swapping in the real calls. Full step-by-step instructions, plus the
+entire fork source and the working Linux build, handed over as
+`steamworks-leaderboard-fork.zip` -- not committed into this repo
+directly, since it's a separate dependency the same way the plain npm
+`steamworks.js` package already is, not part of Guildbound's own
+source.
+
